WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:03.246
- The magic happens when
we're all on the same page

00:00:03.246 --> 00:00:05.281
collaborating and building
something together.

00:00:05.281 --> 00:00:07.685
There's always this
temptation to assume that

00:00:07.685 --> 00:00:10.635
the way things are today is
the way things are going to be.

00:00:10.635 --> 00:00:11.840
We're always sort of experimenting.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.106
The International itself
was an experiment.

00:00:14.106 --> 00:00:15.291
Approach the problem of,

00:00:15.291 --> 00:00:17.250
if I'm a fan and I hear about this,

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.286
it gets me really fired up and excited.

00:00:19.286 --> 00:00:21.526
The rate of innovation is
not remaining constant.

00:00:21.526 --> 00:00:23.202
It's not like we're plateauing.

00:00:23.202 --> 00:00:25.721
In just about every dimension,
innovation is increasing.

00:00:25.721 --> 00:00:27.778
We thrive on the feedback we receive.

00:00:27.778 --> 00:00:31.196
We get better when we
know what people think,

00:00:31.196 --> 00:00:32.522
both positive and negative.

00:00:32.522 --> 00:00:34.094
If you have a limited amount of time,

00:00:34.094 --> 00:00:37.019
that's the scarcest resource we have.

00:00:37.019 --> 00:00:40.005
There wasn't some magic pioneer fairy

00:00:40.005 --> 00:00:42.616
who hit us on the head and
allowed us to do pioneering work.

00:00:42.616 --> 00:00:44.643
But, that's how we evolve.

00:00:44.643 --> 00:00:47.902
Usually, by people deciding
that I have a bad idea.

00:00:47.902 --> 00:00:50.152
(laughing)

00:00:51.536 --> 00:00:53.471
- He's the co-founder
and managing director

00:00:53.471 --> 00:00:55.612
of the video game company Valve.

00:00:55.612 --> 00:00:58.217
He spent 13 years working for Microsoft.

00:00:58.217 --> 00:01:00.541
Ultimately, becoming a
Microsoft millionaire.

00:01:00.541 --> 00:01:01.880
According to Forbes Magazine,

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:05.074
he has a net worth of 2.2 billion dollars.

00:01:05.074 --> 00:01:09.340
He's Gabe Newell and here are
his top 10 rules for success.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:11.783
(explosion)

00:01:11.783 --> 00:01:14.194
- You're testing your ideas,
you're stimulating each other.

00:01:14.194 --> 00:01:15.876
That's what makes us successful.

00:01:15.876 --> 00:01:19.488
It's not some flash of
brilliance by somebody

00:01:19.488 --> 00:01:23.324
who then codes 24 hours a day for 3 weeks.

00:01:23.324 --> 00:01:26.766
The magic happens when
we're all on the same page

00:01:26.766 --> 00:01:29.227
collaborating and building
something together.

00:01:29.227 --> 00:01:31.526
There's always this
temptation to assume that

00:01:31.526 --> 00:01:35.502
the way things are today is
the way things are going to be

00:01:35.502 --> 00:01:38.919
and having been through this long enough,

00:01:39.828 --> 00:01:42.379
in the games industry,

00:01:42.379 --> 00:01:45.962
I think I, and everyone
at Valve, know that

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:51.576
you're only as successful
as what you've done lately.

00:01:51.576 --> 00:01:55.576
So, the idea that Steam
is somehow the anything,

00:01:59.227 --> 00:02:01.628
the answer to digital distribution,

00:02:01.628 --> 00:02:04.861
I think ignores the fact
that every two or three years

00:02:04.861 --> 00:02:06.857
something is going to change dramatically.

00:02:06.857 --> 00:02:08.738
It's like, along comes the Wii

00:02:08.738 --> 00:02:11.508
and that overthrows a bunch of ideas.

00:02:11.508 --> 00:02:14.437
Along comes social gaming
and that throws over

00:02:14.437 --> 00:02:17.286
a bunch of ideas about
what things are like.

00:02:17.286 --> 00:02:18.827
You know, I think Steam is the same way.

00:02:18.827 --> 00:02:22.341
It's like, if you stand still
and if you're not looking

00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:25.807
to the future and doing
the things that you need to

00:02:25.807 --> 00:02:30.346
to be valuable in the future
to customers and partners,

00:02:30.346 --> 00:02:32.271
you're going to get left
behind really rapidly.

00:02:32.271 --> 00:02:34.253
So, that's what we focus in on,

00:02:34.253 --> 00:02:35.835
is what do we need to do next.

00:02:35.835 --> 00:02:38.260
What are the tools and
services and features

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:41.131
and capabilities to make gamers happy

00:02:41.131 --> 00:02:43.312
and other software developers happy.

00:02:43.312 --> 00:02:46.386
We look at all of our games
and evaluate them in terms of

00:02:46.386 --> 00:02:48.704
what we think would be
best for the community.

00:02:48.704 --> 00:02:50.078
You know, we're always
sort of experimenting.

00:02:50.078 --> 00:02:52.747
The International itself
was an experiment.

00:02:52.747 --> 00:02:56.169
You know, Counter Strike was an experiment

00:02:56.169 --> 00:02:57.535
all the way back at the beginning

00:02:57.535 --> 00:03:01.250
where we were like, maybe we
can do an online only game.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:04.301
So, we're always sort
of looking for each game

00:03:04.301 --> 00:03:06.083
will sort of leap frog the other.

00:03:06.083 --> 00:03:08.969
Then what we learn goes back
to all of our other games.

00:03:08.969 --> 00:03:10.835
- [Interviewer] Of course,
your first game was Half Life,

00:03:10.835 --> 00:03:12.462
which was very successful.

00:03:12.462 --> 00:03:13.700
What was the key to that?

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.184
- I think part of the reason

00:03:16.184 --> 00:03:19.545
that the community responded to it

00:03:19.545 --> 00:03:22.545
is we approached the game like fans.

00:03:24.471 --> 00:03:28.638
We wanted to ... It seemed like
people were looking at what

00:03:30.878 --> 00:03:32.941
id had been creating with Doom and Quake

00:03:32.941 --> 00:03:36.363
and had picked out some of
the least interesting details

00:03:36.363 --> 00:03:38.202
of those games to build upon.

00:03:38.202 --> 00:03:41.452
If a double-barrelled shotgun was good,

00:03:43.516 --> 00:03:45.503
a four-barrelled shotgun was better

00:03:45.503 --> 00:03:49.816
and what we had thought when
we were playing the game was,

00:03:49.816 --> 00:03:51.741
there was so much atmosphere

00:03:51.741 --> 00:03:55.474
and so much environmental story telling

00:03:55.474 --> 00:03:57.611
that was going on in these games

00:03:57.611 --> 00:04:00.915
that they sort of opened
the door to this new way,

00:04:00.915 --> 00:04:02.686
this new kind of entertainment,

00:04:02.686 --> 00:04:06.247
and that viewing it as
just a shooting gallery,

00:04:06.247 --> 00:04:09.204
which is what all of the Doom copies and,

00:04:09.204 --> 00:04:12.902
later, Quake copies
seemed to be extracting

00:04:12.902 --> 00:04:15.038
from the success of Doom and Quake,

00:04:15.038 --> 00:04:17.957
was missing the whole
point of what we as fans

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:21.977
had seen that had gotten
us really excited.

00:04:21.977 --> 00:04:24.579
And so, that's what the
impulse was behind our games,

00:04:24.579 --> 00:04:27.011
was to take those pieces as fans

00:04:27.011 --> 00:04:28.583
that we had really loved about those games

00:04:28.583 --> 00:04:30.483
and try to amplify that and build on that

00:04:30.483 --> 00:04:31.879
as much as we could.

00:04:31.879 --> 00:04:34.118
I think it turned out that
we were on the right track,

00:04:34.118 --> 00:04:36.793
that approaching it that
way with sort of a fan's

00:04:36.793 --> 00:04:40.960
enthusiasm and sensibilities
about what was compelling about

00:04:42.154 --> 00:04:45.486
and what the opportunities
were to push even further.

00:04:45.486 --> 00:04:48.078
That ended up really working in our favor

00:04:48.078 --> 00:04:51.983
and I think that that
trying to think like a fan

00:04:51.983 --> 00:04:54.392
is a super valuable thing
to any game developer.

00:04:54.392 --> 00:04:57.197
Don't think about it in terms
of constraints, or budgets,

00:04:57.197 --> 00:05:01.207
or what management somewhere is thinking,

00:05:01.207 --> 00:05:03.222
but approach the problem of, if I'm a fan

00:05:03.222 --> 00:05:04.578
and I hear about this,

00:05:04.578 --> 00:05:06.670
it gets me really fired up and excited.

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:10.837
That's a valuable tool in
approaching this business.

00:05:12.649 --> 00:05:14.529
I get a lot of questions from other

00:05:14.529 --> 00:05:18.179
people who run companies
about organizations.

00:05:18.179 --> 00:05:21.267
I think that if the important
thing that you do is about

00:05:21.267 --> 00:05:22.796
measure-ability and repeatability,

00:05:22.796 --> 00:05:25.253
then hierarchical
organizations are appropriate,

00:05:25.253 --> 00:05:27.597
but that's not what is
happening in our industry.

00:05:27.597 --> 00:05:29.856
The rate of innovation is
not remaining constant.

00:05:29.856 --> 00:05:31.590
It's not like we're plateauing.

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:34.160
In just about every dimension,
innovation is increasing

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:35.925
and the most effective organizations

00:05:35.925 --> 00:05:38.200
are those that can self-organize

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:40.533
and that are extremely flat.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:43.828
And that the question, when
you're trying to think about

00:05:43.828 --> 00:05:47.587
how your company is organized
or how you want it to grow,

00:05:47.587 --> 00:05:49.893
is how you can help people deliver,

00:05:49.893 --> 00:05:51.931
smart and creative people
create the most value

00:05:51.931 --> 00:05:54.908
and delivered as efficiently
as possible to your customers.

00:05:54.908 --> 00:05:56.498
The decision to break into episodes

00:05:56.498 --> 00:06:00.308
wasn't really driven by
the story requirements.

00:06:00.308 --> 00:06:03.086
The story sort of ended
being fit into three buckets.

00:06:03.086 --> 00:06:04.830
Typically, in any of these larger arcs,

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:05.973
it's not too surprising

00:06:05.973 --> 00:06:08.179
that you can think of them as three acts

00:06:08.179 --> 00:06:11.035
and that's a very
traditional Hollywood-esque

00:06:11.035 --> 00:06:13.647
approach to story telling.

00:06:13.647 --> 00:06:16.630
The decision to go with
episodics was more driven out of

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:18.804
the experience we had
working on Half Life 2,

00:06:18.804 --> 00:06:21.750
where we really weren't
able to give anything

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:24.627
to our customers for
like a five year period.

00:06:24.627 --> 00:06:28.381
And, that was very hard
on us as game developers

00:06:28.381 --> 00:06:31.233
because we thrive on
the feedback we receive.

00:06:31.233 --> 00:06:34.669
We get better when we
know what people think,

00:06:34.669 --> 00:06:36.183
both positive and negative

00:06:36.183 --> 00:06:38.683
and so with episodic,
it really allowed us to

00:06:38.683 --> 00:06:40.695
tighten that loop up enormously.

00:06:40.695 --> 00:06:42.983
There are lots of benefits
in terms of managing scale,

00:06:42.983 --> 00:06:46.502
in terms of reacting to what's happening

00:06:46.502 --> 00:06:48.596
on the hardware side,

00:06:48.596 --> 00:06:50.790
but one of the biggest reasons is simply

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:52.207
so we don't get into a situation

00:06:52.207 --> 00:06:55.156
where we have to wait five
years before hearing from

00:06:55.156 --> 00:06:57.503
the people who play our games about

00:06:57.503 --> 00:07:00.100
what worked and what we need to do better.

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:01.676
If you have a limited amount of time,

00:07:01.676 --> 00:07:04.991
that's the scarcest resource we have,

00:07:04.991 --> 00:07:07.829
every decision you make with your time

00:07:07.829 --> 00:07:11.619
is a vote about what the
company's priority should be.

00:07:11.619 --> 00:07:16.239
So the question you should
ask as a programmer is:

00:07:16.239 --> 00:07:18.849
What's the best way for me
to spend my time right now

00:07:18.849 --> 00:07:21.037
that creates value for our customer.

00:07:21.037 --> 00:07:23.985
There wasn't some magic pioneer fairy

00:07:23.985 --> 00:07:26.599
who hit us on the head and
allowed us to do pioneering work.

00:07:26.599 --> 00:07:28.538
It's really a consequence of
a bunch of other decisions

00:07:28.538 --> 00:07:29.838
you make along the way.

00:07:29.838 --> 00:07:31.965
How your games are being funded,

00:07:31.965 --> 00:07:33.675
how you select your colleagues,

00:07:33.675 --> 00:07:35.334
how you organize your teams,

00:07:35.334 --> 00:07:37.538
are really the things that
are going to allow you

00:07:37.538 --> 00:07:39.876
to do pioneering work.

00:07:39.876 --> 00:07:43.117
In our case, we've designed
the company in such a way

00:07:43.117 --> 00:07:45.659
that we really, not only
give everyone who works there

00:07:45.659 --> 00:07:47.934
the opportunity, but they
all have the expectation

00:07:47.934 --> 00:07:50.682
that they're going to be doing
original and creative work.

00:07:50.682 --> 00:07:54.515
I am a big believer in
one person, one office.

00:07:55.528 --> 00:07:58.879
DeMarco's Peopleware, if
anybody has read that,

00:07:58.879 --> 00:08:00.941
I sort of treat that as a bible.

00:08:00.941 --> 00:08:03.788
And so I was like, I hated cubicles

00:08:03.788 --> 00:08:06.147
with a passion and a vengeance.

00:08:06.147 --> 00:08:09.130
It's like, they grew out of
this data general philosophy,

00:08:09.130 --> 00:08:10.847
of if you're screwing your employees,

00:08:10.847 --> 00:08:14.253
you're probably doing something right.

00:08:14.253 --> 00:08:18.213
So, I was like, okay, everybody
always has their own office.

00:08:18.213 --> 00:08:20.463
And, you know, you've got a

00:08:21.746 --> 00:08:23.195
do not disturb button on your phone

00:08:23.195 --> 00:08:24.452
and you have a door you can close

00:08:24.452 --> 00:08:26.814
and you can decorate your
office however you want

00:08:26.814 --> 00:08:30.938
and people kept sneaking
into other people's offices

00:08:30.938 --> 00:08:32.798
and then they started tearing doors down.

00:08:32.798 --> 00:08:34.656
So, everybody had their own office,

00:08:34.656 --> 00:08:36.618
but nobody was ever in it.

00:08:36.618 --> 00:08:39.119
So, they were all congregating
in our conference rooms

00:08:39.119 --> 00:08:40.747
and so we had all these empty offices

00:08:40.747 --> 00:08:44.914
and all these clusters of
people in conference rooms

00:08:46.735 --> 00:08:50.330
and that's when I realized, you know,

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:52.202
they were making the right decision.

00:08:52.202 --> 00:08:57.073
They were deciding that
there were benefits to being

00:08:57.073 --> 00:08:59.108
in closer proximity to other people

00:08:59.108 --> 00:09:03.517
and that Gabe's rigid adherence
to one person, one office

00:09:03.517 --> 00:09:06.828
was actually hurting their productivity.

00:09:06.828 --> 00:09:10.548
So, even after being hit
on the head long enough,

00:09:10.548 --> 00:09:14.708
I realized that everybody
else was making a decision

00:09:14.708 --> 00:09:18.521
that was correct for them
and so now we just say

00:09:18.521 --> 00:09:21.331
everybody designs their own office space.

00:09:21.331 --> 00:09:23.646
So, our largest single room, right now,

00:09:23.646 --> 00:09:25.813
has about 80 people in it.

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:31.245
I share an office with two other people.

00:09:31.245 --> 00:09:34.661
That's one of the things you think about.

00:09:34.661 --> 00:09:36.778
You think about how you
communicate with other people.

00:09:36.778 --> 00:09:41.540
You think about what's an
optimal work environment

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:44.681
for the goals that you have.

00:09:44.681 --> 00:09:46.631
Desks are all on wheels.

00:09:46.631 --> 00:09:48.628
We only have two contact
points with the floor.

00:09:48.628 --> 00:09:51.837
So, you can pack up and join
a different group of people

00:09:51.837 --> 00:09:53.504
in about 15 minutes.

00:09:56.382 --> 00:09:58.411
Some people are working on the design of,

00:09:58.411 --> 00:10:00.127
if anybody's done paired programming,

00:10:00.127 --> 00:10:01.881
there are some people who
are trying to build a desk

00:10:01.881 --> 00:10:04.020
that's more optimal for paired programming

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:05.806
and giving you a shared thing.

00:10:05.806 --> 00:10:08.274
And, they have to make a
decision whether it's more worth

00:10:08.274 --> 00:10:11.440
their time to work on something like that

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:13.416
than it is to do the work
that they already are.

00:10:13.416 --> 00:10:15.001
So, we just sort of said, okay,

00:10:15.001 --> 00:10:18.179
each person has to make
their own decisions about

00:10:18.179 --> 00:10:20.128
what they work, who they're working with,

00:10:20.128 --> 00:10:21.474
what are the tools they need,

00:10:21.474 --> 00:10:25.477
and what their offices ought to look like.

00:10:25.477 --> 00:10:27.431
But, that's how we evolve.

00:10:27.431 --> 00:10:31.598
Usually, by people deciding
that I have a bad idea.

00:10:33.347 --> 00:10:35.990
- And this wonderful mask.

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:38.657
(crowd talking)

00:10:48.086 --> 00:10:49.781
Alright, thanks Gabe.

00:10:49.781 --> 00:10:50.614
- Thanks.

00:10:51.668 --> 00:10:53.150
- Can I get a picture of you as well.

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:54.103
- Sure.

00:10:54.103 --> 00:10:57.159
- [Audience Member] Put on the mask.

00:10:57.159 --> 00:10:57.992
- I'll put on the mask.

00:10:57.992 --> 00:10:58.825
- Alright.

00:11:00.294 --> 00:11:02.544
(laughing)

00:11:12.898 --> 00:11:16.231
- [Audience] Three, three, three, three.

00:11:17.176 --> 00:11:19.426
(laughing)

00:11:24.979 --> 00:11:26.123
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:26.123 --> 00:11:29.766
I made this video because
Kinaru Helio asked me to.

00:11:29.766 --> 00:11:31.019
So, if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:11:31.019 --> 00:11:33.017
that you want me to profile next,

00:11:33.017 --> 00:11:35.347
leave it in the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:11:35.347 --> 00:11:37.267
I'd also love to know
which of Gabe Newell's

00:11:37.267 --> 00:11:39.203
top 10 rules meant the most to you,

00:11:39.203 --> 00:11:41.113
had the biggest impact on your life.

00:11:41.113 --> 00:11:43.275
Leave it in the comments and
I'll join in the discussion.

00:11:43.275 --> 00:11:44.492
Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:44.492 --> 00:11:48.314
Continue to believe and I'll see you soon.

00:11:48.314 --> 00:11:49.644
(explosion)

